===============================================================================
1.  THE DEFECT
===============================================================================

askExpectedValue opens with `(void)target;` -- v04.hpp:435, inherited verbatim at
v05.hpp:427 -- so the value half of the ask score cannot see who is being asked.
Every target-dependent quantity anywhere in the ask score is material: hit
probability, reply threat, hand size, exposure, follow-up hit probability
(P5-verify-target-channel.md section 1).  Measured over 154,318 v0.4-mirror ask
decisions, 46.6% offer at least two hard-indistinguishable holders of the card
actually asked for, worth a mean 0.639 bits -- 43.9% and 0.607 bits at seed
777001, 66.2% and 0.919 bits against v0.3 -- roughly 41 bits per team per game
that no term consumes (P5-human-strategy.md section 3A, P5-verify-target-channel
section 2).

The verification report corrects the PRICE, and the correction shaped this
design.  Inside that class the spread of v0.4's own posterior is 0.25-0.29, not
the 0.081 of the capacity-marginal yardstick, and the intra-class spread of the
total ask score is 3.0-3.4, exceeding v0.4's top1-top2 margin at 86-88% of such
decisions (P5-verify-target-channel.md section 3).  The channel is priced, not
free.  The same report also measures that the value part supplies 1.9% of the
score variation that selects an ask against the linear part's 98.1% (section 4).

  => Consequence for this patch: the target dimension is NOT folded into
     askExpectedValue.  Doing so would expose a 0.64-0.92 bit channel to the
     weakest half of the score.  `(void)target;` stays, with a comment saying
     why, and the target dimension is priced by its own explicitly weighted term
     at the top level of chooseAsk, on the linear score's scale.

===============================================================================
2.  M4 -- WHAT WAS BUILT, AND ONE CORRECTION TO THE DESIGN NOTE
===============================================================================

DESIGN.md M4 and the task budget "6x the bookkeeping".  It is 1x.

The public deduction state is COMMON KNOWLEDGE.  In Knowledge::onEvent
(belief.hpp:153-210) every write to owner/mask/unresolved/disj/askCount/
missCount/totalAsks/handCount/publicKnown is a function of the event alone; the
only two lines that consult `me` are belief.hpp:207-208, which maintain myHand.
Six objects fed the same stream from an all-possible start therefore agree bit
for bit outside me/myHand.  probe_m45_test.cpp check D asserts this empirically
rather than resting on the code reading: 0 mismatches in 12,355 comparisons
(25 games, seed 31, v05 mirror).

So M4 maintains ONE incremental public object and derives a per-seat model
lazily:

    model(j) = public state, refined by every card OUR OWN k proves seat j holds.

That refinement is what makes the models differ between seats at all -- the
public state alone can only rank opponents by our belief about their cards, not
by their knowledge -- and it is sound as a model of j's knowledge because j knows
its own hand.

LIMITATION, stated plainly because it changes how the output may be used.
model(j) omits constraint C1 for seat j: every deduction j makes from the part of
j's hand we cannot see.  It is a STRICT UNDER-APPROXIMATION.  Measured: the model
resolves 0.124-0.152 of (observer, seat, card) triples against the seat's own
0.274-0.298 -- it sees roughly HALF of what the seat itself has resolved.
Therefore:

  (i)  every fact in model(j) is genuinely known to j, so it is a sound LOWER
       BOUND on opponent knowledge.  `owner[c] == u` in model(t) is a
       certificate: "opponent t provably knows our seat u holds card c".  That is
       the direction blackballing needs, which is why the hard lockout term is a
       proof and not an estimate.
  (ii) it can NEVER be used the other way.  model(t) not knowing something is not
       evidence that t does not know it.  Nothing in this patch rewards an ask
       for being unreadable, and nothing treats an ask as safe because the model
       is ignorant of it.  If a later mechanism (M3's leak term is the obvious
       candidate) wants that direction, it needs a different object.

===============================================================================
3.  M5 -- THE FOUR PARTS
===============================================================================

(a) LOCKOUT / BLACKBALL.  v0.5's f[8] = (1-p)*threatOf(target) estimates the
    target's CARDS and fudges its knowledge with `0.7 + 0.3*min(1, askCount/3)`
    (v05.hpp:211).  Develin's blackball turns on the opponent KNOWING where a
    card is.  M5a scores that from the target's own model:

      value(t) = max over live half-suits S, cards c of S,
                     P(t can legally ask in S) * P_t(c is on our team)

    with P_t read off model(t) (capacity-weighted, no dynamic program).  When
    model(t) has c resolved to one of our seats the second factor is exactly 1
    and the whole term is a certificate.  Measured: a hard certificate exists at
    85.1% of decisions, at strength >0.5 for 67.7% and >0.9 for 37.5%
    (25 games seed 31, v05 mirror; 85.2/62.2/29.1 for the v04 mirror at seed
    90210, 82.8/61.0/18.3 for the v04 mirror at seed 31).

    m5aPostMiss additionally prices the certificate THIS ask would emit: a miss
    tells the table "the asker holds another card of S" (C5, belief.hpp:158-171),
    and if that disjunction has exactly one survivor in the target's model, the
    target learns precisely which card we hold and takes it with the turn we just
    handed over.  That is Develin's worked example.  First-order only: the
    cascades from the two exclusions are not chased, because that would need a
    Knowledge copy and an onEvent per candidate ask.

(b) VOID PROGRESS.  Taking an opponent's last card of a half-suit permanently
    removes their right to ask in it -- legalAsk requires
    `g.hand[actor] & setMask(s)` (fish.hpp:164).  v0.5's f[11] fires only at
    handCount[target]==1, which is emptying the whole hand, not voiding a
    half-suit.  M5b adds P(this ask takes the target's last card of S), scaled by
    our team's stake in S.  An opportunity worth >0.25 exists at 26.9% of v0.5
    mirror decisions (14.8% for the v0.4 mirror).

(c) LAST-LIVE-OPPONENT TRAP.  f[11] is split.  Emptying one of several opponents
    stays in f[11]; emptying the LAST live opponent triggers the forced endgame
    and becomes two features, the upside (with the opposing team cardless every
    remaining card is ours) and the downside (we must then allocate every
    remaining half-suit blind), so the fit can price them separately.

    HONEST NEGATIVE: this is insurance, not a scorer.  A last-live-opponent ask
    is available at only 1.15-1.39% of decisions (1.39% v05 mirror, 1.15-1.16%
    v04 mirror), and in a 20-game smoke test
    m5c=1 alone reproduced the all-off result exactly.  M1 and M2 have already
    removed most forced endgames.  Budget nothing for it; keep it because the
    failure it guards against cost v0.4 100% of 28 forced declarations.

(d) CODEBOOK ON THE TARGET INDEX.  Construction B of signalling.md 7.9, behind
    `conventions=1`, OFF by default per owner decision D1.  Opponents at relative
    seat offsets +1/+3 address teammates at +2/+4; the third opponent is reserved
    as "no frame, material ask only".  Relative offsets, not absolute seats, so
    the codebook means the same thing in every rotation of a duplicate block.

    LIMITATION: sender side only.  The receiver-side decode belongs with M3 and
    is not built here, so M5d alone should measure at approximately zero.  It is
    included so the machinery and the D1 flag exist, not because it will score.

===============================================================================
4.  SWITCHES AND WEIGHTS
===============================================================================

    v05:m4=0        do not build the per-seat models (disables m5a and m5d)
    v05:m5a=0       no lockout term
    v05:m5b=0       no void-progress term
    v05:m5c=0       restore v0.4's undifferentiated f[11]
    v05:m5d=1,conventions=1     Construction B codebook
    v05:m5ahard=1   lockout scored only where it is a certificate
    v05:m5apost=0   drop the certificate this ask would emit
    v05:m5af8=1     zero f[8], so the card-based threat does not double count
    v05:mw0..mw4    the five M5 weights

THE WEIGHTS IN M45Config ARE UNFITTED PLACEHOLDERS, scale-matched to the existing
fitted vector (lockout takes f[8]'s -3.0978, the last-live upside keeps f[11]'s
+1.1660).  They are not measured and no strength claim may be made from them.
The factory hunk extends the flat `allparams` vector by NM45 entries at K+14..
K+18 so `fish tune` fits them with everything else; a vector produced before M4/M5
existed is shorter and leaves them untouched, so old fits still load.

Because m5c changes what f[11] MEANS, w[11] must be refit alongside mw[].  The
round-1 fit running now (research/v05/runs/fit-round1.jsonl) is a fit of the
pre-M4/M5 v0.5 and remains valid as that; it is not a fit of this configuration.

CAUTION, and this is the practical one.  The placeholder weights are NOT neutral.
In a 40-game smoke test against v0.4 (seed 4242, no duplicate rotations, far too
small to mean anything on its own) the all-off build scored 171-189 in sets and
the placeholder-weight build 170-190.  Do not switch M4/M5 on in a headline
configuration before the refit.  The defaults ship the mechanisms ON only so that
the ablation harness has something to turn OFF; if that is the wrong default for
the lead session, set m5a=m5b=m5c=0 in M45Config and the build is byte-for-byte
shipped v0.5 (verified below).

