5.  VERIFICATION RUN THIS SESSION
===============================================================================

Soundness (engine/src/probe_m45_test.cpp; the audit idea of Game::runAudit,
game.hpp:120-146, applied to the derived models):

    build:  c++ -std=c++20 -O2 -Isrc src/probe_m45_test.cpp -o m45test -pthread
    runs:   ./m45test --games=25 --seed=31     --a=v05 --b=v05
            ./m45test --games=25 --seed=90210  --a=v04 --b=v04
            ./m45test --games=15 --seed=31     --a=v04 --b=v04
            ./m45test --games=15 --seed=777001 --a=v05 --b=v04
            ./m45test --games=15 --seed=90210  --a=v05 --b=v03

    pooled over the five runs -- 95 games, 3 seeds, 4 matchups, 25.1M checks:

    A  model excludes the true holder of a card   0 of 13,777,560
    B  capacity mismatches                        0 of  2,414,232
    C  unsatisfied C5 certificates                0 of  6,558,355
    D  common-knowledge mismatches                0 of     55,885
    E  model of self stronger than own knowledge  0 of  2,296,260
    F  build contradictions                       0
    every run also passes the pre-existing Game::runAudit with 0 violations.

Cost, per ask decision (25 games, seed 31, v05 mirror):

    public model onEvent      64 ns   (a real agent's own Knowledge::onEvent,
                                       fed the same stream: 91 ns -- so M4's
                                       public bookkeeping is CHEAPER than the
                                       update the agent already performs)
    lazy per-seat build      144 ns
    beginDecision           1127 ns + 1772 ns to score 46.1 candidates
    ONE Sinkhorn solve      5488 ns, and v0.5 already pays 13 per decision
    => M4 + M5 overhead      4.1% of the posterior work already done
                             (2.4% on the v0.4 mirror, whose posteriors are
                              slower: 2,566 ns against 13 x 8,189 ns)

    The per-event control was corrected after review: it previously timed a
    SECOND public Knowledge (the check-D objects), which compares a public model
    against a public model and says nothing about the agent's own cost.  It now
    times a shadow copy of the live agent's `k`, resynced every event, so the
    control carries constraint C1 the way the agent's knowledge does.  Timings
    are load-sensitive -- these were taken while a parameter fit occupied every
    core -- but the soundness and population counts below are exact and
    reproducible.

Does M5 use the channel it was built for?  Among (decision, card) pairs with two
or more targets the hard certificates cannot separate, M5's score strictly
separates them at 99.6% (v0.5 mirror) / 99.8% (v0.4 mirror); restricted to the
best card by hit probability, 97.3% / 98.6%.  NOTE this is a different population
from P5's 46.6%: P5 measured the card v0.4 ACTUALLY asked, and 36.6% of those
were provably dead, which collapses the class to one member.

Regression: with m4=0,m5a=0,m5b=0,m5c=0,m5d=0 the patched policy reproduces
shipped v0.5 EXACTLY -- identical sets, wins, events/game and declaration
accuracy over 40 games vs v04 and 40 games mirror (seed 4242).

NOT MEASURED, and deliberately not claimed: whether M4/M5 make v0.5 stronger.
That needs the ablation match after a refit that includes mw[], and the standing
preference applies -- per-opponent breakdown, explicit worst case across styles,
exploitability probe, never an aggregate win rate alone.

